Here's my two cents worth: I don't think "doing something productive"
means running your computer idle over a year. Besides, Windows 98
hasn't been sold as professional tool, instead it is for the consumer
market.

Operating system itself means little when doing something productive,
unless you are working as administrator/IT support etc. The software
that  is run under an operating system affects more to being
productive. Of course, if computer crashes, it affects productivety
from time to time. But if you can't find good tool for certain OS, it
affects your productivety all the time.

So, the question "which OS is best?" is very subjective - for example I
couldn't find the tools that I need for Linux. For me Windows and Mac
OS offers greates variety and quality of software tools to choose from.
